# function Accepts(req) { this.headers = req.headers }   class Message { constructor(held) { this.held = held } get headers() { return this.held } }   function reads(req) { return req.headers }
# What is expected of `req` is what its uses say: something with `headers`. It comes as the family of that type: the layouts with a FIELD of the name: Accepts and Held. A Message answers the name too, from above: the family is known to be
# short, and a test for it would fail for every Message: nothing is expected by it. Where no class answers from above (f5: `negotiator`) the family is expected as before. A written type is not asked (f6).
# f1 = Accepts, f3 = the getter, f4 = reads, f5 = readsOther, f6 = readsTyped, f7 = Held
# RUN: walk f4:Body mechanisms=all-ParameterGuards
# CHECK-LABEL: code f4:Body
# CHECK-NOT: ShapeTest
# CHECK: Return
# RUN(OTHER): walk f5:Body mechanisms=all-ParameterGuards
# OTHER-LABEL: code f5:Body
# OTHER: ShapeTest {{*}}layouts=[L0, L2]
# RUN(TYPED): walk f6:Body mechanisms=all-ParameterGuards
# TYPED-LABEL: code f6:Body
# TYPED: ShapeTest {{*}}layouts=[L0, L2]
# The `this` of a method is named by its class, not by its uses: it is not asked (f8).
# RUN(THIS): walk f8:Body mechanisms=all-ParameterGuards
# THIS-LABEL: code f8:Body
# THIS: ShapeTest {{*}}layouts=[L0, L2]

